1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Four and a half acres of floating military might.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Enough brute force to move massive ships across the globe.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:14,000
A mammoth 300-foot fire-breathing metal melting monster.

4
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:19,000
An unprecedented design that blew the roof off the traditional sports stadium.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
And giant robots taking over the world.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Forget what you've heard. Size does matter.

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, World's Biggest Machines on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The USS Ronald Reagan is the biggest and most technologically advanced aircraft carrier in the world.

9
00:00:53,001 --> 00:01:02,000
Also known as CVN 76, she is the ninth ship in the Nimitz class, the largest designation of flattop.

10
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:18,000
USS Ronald Reagan, length 1,092 feet, flight deck area 4.5 acres, aircraft capacity 80, personnel capacity 6,000.

11
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:27,240
Powered by two nuclear reactors and driven by four bronze propellers weighing over 66,000 pounds each,

12
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:31,320
the USS Ronald Reagan reaches speeds of over 30 knots.

13
00:01:34,060 --> 00:01:36,640
All the Nimitz-class ships are almost identical.

14
00:01:37,460 --> 00:01:40,200
What makes this one the biggest is its flight deck.

15
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:46,560
The deck angle has increased from 9.05 degrees to 9.15 degrees from the center line.

16
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:53,560
This allows unobstructed simultaneous launching and recovering of aircraft.

17
00:01:53,561 --> 00:01:57,560
Home to the men and women who take to the seas to defend the United States,

18
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:02,560
CVN 76 is practically a floating city.

19
00:02:02,561 --> 00:02:04,560
While underway during their employment cycle,

20
00:02:04,561 --> 00:02:08,560
there is little or no access to outside provisions and services.

21
00:02:08,561 --> 00:02:11,560
Self-sufficiency is crucial.

22
00:02:11,561 --> 00:02:13,560
During that 15 months of employability,

23
00:02:13,561 --> 00:02:16,220
will be ready to go wherever the nation needs us to go.

24
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:20,080
That might come in the form of two months here,

25
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:24,860
two months there, four months total, six months total.

26
00:02:25,240 --> 00:02:27,780
It will be whatever we need to do over that 15-month cycle.

27
00:02:28,660 --> 00:02:32,940
Once at sea, the carrier requires 24-7 operation

28
00:02:32,941 --> 00:02:38,160
to sustain the large crew, maintain the ship, aircraft and arsenal,

29
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,080
and, of course, perform the assigned military tasks.

30
00:02:42,081 --> 00:02:49,080
We can hold about 6,000 people at one time, about half of that for the ship's crew and about half of that for the aircraft community,

31
00:02:49,960 --> 00:02:52,400
which means we have to serve about 18,000 meals a day.

32
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:55,680
It all has to be done very efficiently and fast so everybody can get back to their job assignments.

33
00:02:56,180 --> 00:03:02,180
She's got about 2,000 telephones and about 8 million feet of cable intertwined inside to make all the systems work.

34
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:09,080
But it takes much more than food and phones to run an entire floating city with all the comforts of home.

35
00:03:09,081 --> 00:03:16,080
The USS Ronald Reagan comes complete with most of the amenities you'd find on Main Street, USA.

36
00:03:16,081 --> 00:03:22,080
USS Ronald Reagan is an all-encompassing city, complete with our own police department,

37
00:03:22,081 --> 00:03:30,080
our own mayor who is a commanding officer, our own hospitals, our own laundry and dry-cleaning services on board.

38
00:03:30,081 --> 00:03:34,080
We have our own TV station, our own movie theater, and I wish we had our own bowling alley.

39
00:03:34,081 --> 00:03:39,675
alley. We have our own legal department, fire department, our own airport.

40
00:03:39,676 --> 00:03:40,960
That goes without

41
00:03:40,961 --> 00:03:41,661
saying.

42
00:03:41,662 --> 00:03:48,740
They even have a stocked library, a museum, and their own version of the Hollywood Walk of

43
00:03:48,741 --> 00:03:49,441
Fame.

44
00:03:49,442 --> 00:03:55,880
The Ronald Reagan is also a completely cashless ship, where crew conduct all their

45
00:03:55,881 --> 00:04:00,199
banking and make onboard purchases with just the swipe of a card.

46
00:04:00,200 --> 00:04:01,860
We are one of the world's

47
00:04:01,861 --> 00:04:07,960
largest gas stations carrying over three million gallons of fuel to supply all of our aircraft that

48
00:04:07,961 --> 00:04:08,881
we have on board.

49
00:04:08,882 --> 00:04:12,780
We also are capable of making all of our own electricity, our own water.

50
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:18,529
We make approximately 400,000 gallons of water on a daily basis.

51
00:04:18,530 --> 00:04:20,980
All of this to ensure that the

52
00:04:20,981 --> 00:04:25,613
crew and ship are ready and able to carry out their missions.

53
00:04:25,614 --> 00:04:28,120
Since its inception, the aircraft

54
00:04:28,121 --> 00:04:30,580
carrier has become an essential part of the war machine.

55
00:04:30,581 --> 00:04:37,420
The history of the aircraft carrier starts out in 1920 when a cruiser was converted into

56
00:04:37,421 --> 00:04:38,420
an aircraft carrier.

57
00:04:38,421 --> 00:04:45,240
That was the cruiser Jupiter, which became CV-1 USS Langley.

58
00:04:45,241 --> 00:04:51,540
Since that time, there have been 56 aircraft carriers.

59
00:04:51,541 --> 00:04:56,680
Although we are CVN 76, you'd think there would be 76 of them.

60
00:04:56,681 --> 00:05:00,180
There were some that were started and were not completed.

61
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:07,360
In June of 1942, aircraft carriers were really put to the test

62
00:05:07,361 --> 00:05:12,140
and proved their military worth in a truly historic way.

63
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:17,720
One of the most significant battles that an aircraft carrier was ever in was the Battle of Midway.

64
00:05:17,721 --> 00:05:23,640
It's the first naval engagement where the opposing forces never saw each other.

65
00:05:23,641 --> 00:05:28,889
the Japanese carriers launched their airplanes the U.S.

66
00:05:28,890 --> 00:05:32,420
carriers launched their airplanes and

67
00:05:32,421 --> 00:05:39,860
the whole battle was conducted by the opposing aircraft bombing each other's fleet and engaging

68
00:05:39,861 --> 00:05:48,060
in air-to-air combat dogfights so that was a momentous battle it decimated the Japanese fleet

69
00:05:48,061 --> 00:05:53,960
And we then realized the importance of a carrier and we started creating more of them.

70
00:05:55,780 --> 00:05:59,620
Building aircraft carriers for the U.S. Navy is an enormous undertaking.

71
00:06:01,300 --> 00:06:05,620
Northrop Grumman in Newport News, Virginia, has been doing it for over 70 years.

72
00:06:06,380 --> 00:06:10,520
The first carrier built for the purpose of being a carrier was done in 1934 here.

73
00:06:10,980 --> 00:06:12,000
It was the USS Ranger.

74
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,340
Since then we've gone on to build 26 different carriers.

75
00:06:18,060 --> 00:06:20,900
The Ronald Reagan being the latest and greatest.

76
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:27,320
Designing the USS Ronald Reagan brought historic advances to the world of carrier construction,

77
00:06:28,040 --> 00:06:31,400
employing three-dimensional design concepts and computer modeling.

78
00:06:32,820 --> 00:06:38,180
This allowed the engineers to solve potential problems before they became actual problems.

79
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:45,400
The next phase, construction, required a great deal of labor and patience,

80
00:06:45,401 --> 00:06:48,420
and thousands of tons of steel.

81
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:52,480
Well, the vessel is built in a series of steps.

82
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:54,660
If you think about it like a large erector set,

83
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:58,300
we bring together 1,392 steel blocks

84
00:06:58,301 --> 00:07:02,000
weighing in a range of about 150 to 300 tons.

85
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:04,240
We load those into the dock one at a time,

86
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:06,500
and that makes up the ship assembly.

87
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,860
At the same time, we're putting pipe in,

88
00:07:09,580 --> 00:07:11,160
about a million feet of pipe,

89
00:07:12,060 --> 00:07:13,040
eight million feet of cable,

90
00:07:13,041 --> 00:07:16,060
and we have loaded 47,000 tons of steel into the dock.

91
00:07:17,800 --> 00:07:20,940
All this heavy lifting required some pretty heavy muscle,

92
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:24,280
supplied by another of the world's biggest machines.

93
00:07:25,660 --> 00:07:30,660
This one-of-a-kind gigantic gantry crane can lift up to 900 tons.

94
00:07:32,400 --> 00:07:35,460
Once the pre-built steel block compartments were in

95
00:07:35,461 --> 00:07:37,700
and outfitted with the necessary equipment,

96
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:43,020
Northrop Grumman had to make sure it all worked and looked good.

97
00:07:43,040 --> 00:07:46,040
About 325,000 gallons of paint have to be applied.

98
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:48,300
When we get the hull buttoned up and painted,

99
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:52,580
we then launch the ship after it's been in the dock for about two years.

100
00:07:55,900 --> 00:07:59,900
With over 10 million parts brought in from 48 states,

101
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,880
the USS Ronald Reagan took 45 million man-hours to build

102
00:08:04,881 --> 00:08:07,460
and cost almost $5 billion.

103
00:08:07,461 --> 00:08:08,219
dollars.

104
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:16,000
CVN 76, the Navy's most awesome warship, was like no other aircraft carrier in the

105
00:08:16,001 --> 00:08:16,701
world.

106
00:08:16,702 --> 00:08:21,980
Major innovations have been implemented that would make her a fast and furious force

107
00:08:21,981 --> 00:08:23,620
to be reckoned with.

108
00:08:23,621 --> 00:08:29,440
With each new aircraft carrier that we build, we design in new features

109
00:08:29,441 --> 00:08:31,627
and new facets that make the ship more capable.

110
00:08:31,628 --> 00:08:33,720
We put a bulbous bow on this ship that hasn't

111
00:08:33,721 --> 00:08:37,480
in on any other ships to make it more buoyant and more stable.

112
00:08:37,481 --> 00:08:42,580
We put in a brand new ship self-defense system, a fiber optic system which has revolutionized

113
00:08:42,581 --> 00:08:46,660
the way we carry machinery controls and voice controls throughout the ship.

114
00:08:46,661 --> 00:08:50,340
The island superstructure on this ship was redesigned for better visibility here on the

115
00:08:50,341 --> 00:08:51,340
bridge.

116
00:08:51,341 --> 00:08:56,480
When launching and landing aircraft, it really helps to see where you're going.

117
00:08:56,481 --> 00:09:01,060
USS Ronald Reagan has the biggest pilot house or bridge area among the other Nimitz-class

118
00:09:01,061 --> 00:09:02,220
carriers.

119
00:09:02,221 --> 00:09:05,880
Our bridge is actually extended to the starboard side, which gives us a better navigational

120
00:09:05,881 --> 00:09:09,260
picture as well as more space to work with.

121
00:09:09,261 --> 00:09:15,000
With the latest navigation equipment on board, CVN 76 is on its way to entering the next

122
00:09:15,001 --> 00:09:19,160
evolutionary stage in ship guidance.

123
00:09:19,161 --> 00:09:23,760
The USS Ronald Reagan was designed to be the first paperless carrier, relying solely

124
00:09:23,761 --> 00:09:25,260
on electronic navigation.

125
00:09:25,261 --> 00:09:30,020
A lot of people ask me how it is to steer an aircraft carrier, and I just say that if

126
00:09:30,021 --> 00:09:33,300
If you actually think you're going to steer an aircraft carrier, you're not going to be able to do it.

127
00:09:33,620 --> 00:09:36,220
You have to zone out your surroundings and look at it like a video game.

128
00:09:36,620 --> 00:09:41,220
You have to stay on course and just solely rely on your skills and not worry about anything else.

129
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:47,560
In preparation for the carrier's ultimate purpose, the launching and landing of combat aircraft,

130
00:09:48,500 --> 00:09:52,080
a myriad of tests was performed on the catapult and arresting systems.

131
00:09:52,081 --> 00:09:58,000
to determine correct alignment in steam pressure levels.

132
00:09:58,820 --> 00:10:04,160
Orange sleds, some weighing as much as 90,000 pounds, were used as dummy loads.

133
00:10:05,960 --> 00:10:08,800
Attached to the catapults the same way an actual jet would be,

134
00:10:09,600 --> 00:10:15,640
they were hurled off the flight deck into the James River, over and over again.

135
00:10:15,641 --> 00:10:24,600
On course to its assigned location, the ship may become a floating target and must protect itself.

136
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:32,180
Surface-to-air NATO Sea Sparrow missiles and a rolling airframe missile system for short-range defense,

137
00:10:32,780 --> 00:10:40,400
combined with four 20-millimeter phalanx six-barreled Mk-15s, give the Ronald Reagan some serious firepower.

138
00:10:40,401 --> 00:10:49,080
Despite the grand vessel's imposing presence and overall size, the various compartments

139
00:10:49,081 --> 00:10:51,540
inside are quite small.

140
00:10:51,541 --> 00:10:55,920
It's very difficult any time to live on board because of the close quarters that we're all

141
00:10:55,921 --> 00:10:56,920
in.

142
00:10:56,921 --> 00:11:01,920
You really have to give up your personal issues, like working together with people and being

143
00:11:01,921 --> 00:11:04,300
in such close proximity with them.

144
00:11:04,301 --> 00:11:07,600
It really mends you into more of a team.

145
00:11:07,601 --> 00:11:12,060
And in the end, not only do the people become your family, the ship becomes part of your family also.

146
00:11:13,880 --> 00:11:18,260
Perhaps in the future, there may be less need for so many to live on board an aircraft carrier.

147
00:11:19,380 --> 00:11:22,920
We do have the next design of carrier on the table right now.

148
00:11:23,400 --> 00:11:24,920
Some fairly interesting things there.

149
00:11:25,540 --> 00:11:28,040
But my sense is that the ship will not get much bigger.

150
00:11:28,340 --> 00:11:30,740
And maybe it doesn't need to get too much bigger.

151
00:11:31,360 --> 00:11:35,900
In 50 years, who knows what will be flying off the flight deck of the USS Ronald Reagan.

152
00:11:35,901 --> 00:11:40,220
It may be a mix of manned aircraft and unmanned aerial vehicles.

153
00:11:41,120 --> 00:11:44,160
While the size of the future carrier is yet undetermined,

154
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:47,380
its deadly purpose has always been certain.

155
00:11:47,760 --> 00:11:52,940
The mission of an aircraft carrier is to take the fight to our enemies,

156
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:54,940
wherever they are all over the world,

157
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:57,820
and do so from international waters,

158
00:11:58,440 --> 00:12:03,260
and basically take this four and a half acres of sovereign American territory

159
00:12:03,261 --> 00:12:07,800
and do with it what we will to shape world events in our best interests.

160
00:12:08,520 --> 00:12:13,600
I like to say that our real main job is to drop bombs

161
00:12:13,601 --> 00:12:17,300
to change people's way of looking at the world.

162
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:20,740
Fairly frankly and bluntly put, that's our purpose.

163
00:12:22,640 --> 00:12:25,860
But even the USS Ronald Reagan wouldn't be possible

164
00:12:25,861 --> 00:12:28,500
without another of the world's biggest machines,

165
00:12:28,900 --> 00:12:30,480
a behemoth blast furnace.

166
00:12:30,481 --> 00:12:37,480
The USS Ronald Reagan will need to be refueled just once in its projected 50-year lifespan.

167
00:12:37,481 --> 00:12:42,480
World's Biggest Machines will return on Modern Marvels.

168
00:12:42,481 --> 00:12:48,480
We now return to World's Biggest Machines on Modern Marvels.

169
00:13:00,480 --> 00:13:07,480
In East Chicago, Indiana, there lives a giant, fiery beast.

170
00:13:07,481 --> 00:13:15,480
It's 30 stories tall, has a belly full of flames, and when lanced, bleeds molten metal.

171
00:13:15,481 --> 00:13:24,480
The Beast, a Goliath blast furnace affectionately known as Number 7, is the pride of the Middle Steel Company.

172
00:13:24,481 --> 00:13:27,460
The blast furnace is the first step in the steel making process.

173
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:34,140
Its role is to convert raw iron ore, which is a mixture of iron ore and oxygen and other impurities.

174
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:43,080
The blast furnace melts the ore and it purifies the ore into a liquid metal that is 95% iron content.

175
00:13:43,081 --> 00:13:52,940
Number 7 blast furnace, height 310 feet, consumes 20,000 tons of raw materials per day,

176
00:13:52,941 --> 00:13:57,560
produces 11,000 tons of liquid iron per day.

177
00:13:58,060 --> 00:14:01,740
Blast temperature 2300 degrees Fahrenheit.

178
00:14:03,180 --> 00:14:07,160
Built in 1980 by Inland Steel at a cost of $1 billion,

179
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:11,240
number seven is the largest blast furnace in North America

180
00:14:11,241 --> 00:14:15,480
and the latest triumph in the quest to produce mass quantities of steel

181
00:14:15,481 --> 00:14:17,240
with the utmost efficiency.

182
00:14:19,080 --> 00:14:22,020
And to make steel, you need iron.

183
00:14:22,021 --> 00:14:24,200
Lots and lots of iron.

184
00:14:27,220 --> 00:14:33,780
The pursuit to harness and employ the versatile properties of iron dates back almost 5,000 years.

185
00:14:36,160 --> 00:14:40,400
Mankind has been using iron since about 3,000 B.C.

186
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:45,100
Archaeologists have found iron tools in the pyramids in Egypt.

187
00:14:46,040 --> 00:14:50,660
The first iron-making processes only produced a lump of hot iron

188
00:14:50,661 --> 00:14:54,980
that then had to be taken to a blacksmith forge and hammered into shape.

189
00:14:55,220 --> 00:14:56,620
They did not make liquid iron.

190
00:14:58,320 --> 00:15:00,720
A blast furnace produces liquid iron.

191
00:15:01,400 --> 00:15:06,700
The first blast furnace was developed in Belgium in the mid-1300s A.D.

192
00:15:06,701 --> 00:15:10,840
These first blast furnaces were made of stone instead of steel.

193
00:15:10,841 --> 00:15:16,920
The wind was provided by a water wheel connected to a wooden and leather bellows,

194
00:15:16,921 --> 00:15:19,840
and the liquid iron was casted into molds.

195
00:15:19,841 --> 00:15:23,840
So it was a very simple affair and only made about one net ton per day.

196
00:15:23,841 --> 00:15:27,840
The first stone furnaces used charcoal for fuel.

197
00:15:27,841 --> 00:15:35,840
In the early 1700s, coke, made from abundant coal, took its place to the joy of trees everywhere.

198
00:15:35,841 --> 00:15:41,000
And about 30 years later, a hot blast made the slow process fast.

199
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:47,040
By putting hot compressed air in the furnace, it reduced the amount of fuel you needed in the furnace

200
00:15:47,041 --> 00:15:50,660
and increased the amount of iron ore you could charge.

201
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:53,500
Therefore, the production rate could be increased.

202
00:15:53,501 --> 00:15:58,360
The larger volume of air that you inject into the blast furnace,

203
00:15:58,540 --> 00:16:02,700
the more coke you burn, the more ore you melt, the more iron that you make.

204
00:16:02,701 --> 00:16:09,700
Bigger is better, which is why number seven is a state-of-the-art facility that has perfected

205
00:16:09,701 --> 00:16:12,920
the science of liquid iron production.

206
00:16:12,921 --> 00:16:17,600
The process begins as carefully controlled amounts of raw materials are carried up by

207
00:16:17,601 --> 00:16:21,700
conveyor and charged into the top of the blast furnace.

208
00:16:21,701 --> 00:16:28,020
The iron ore pellets and sinter, which removes impurities, are combined with coke, the fuel.

209
00:16:28,021 --> 00:16:32,620
Just like baking a cake, we have to have so much of each ingredient to make the quality

210
00:16:32,621 --> 00:16:35,020
of iron that is desired by our customer.

211
00:16:35,021 --> 00:16:47,900
We're on the furnace top, which is about 30 stories up from ground level.

212
00:16:47,901 --> 00:16:50,980
All material travels through this point.

213
00:16:50,981 --> 00:16:57,600
Behind me is the boiler house, which is used to supply the compressed hot wind to the furnace.

214
00:16:57,601 --> 00:17:02,134
travels to the furnace via the pipe that you see below you.

215
00:17:02,135 --> 00:17:04,900
At the same time we are charging raw

216
00:17:04,901 --> 00:17:10,080
materials in the top of the furnace, we are putting compressed air in the bottom of the

217
00:17:10,081 --> 00:17:12,846
furnace through a series of nozzles called tweers.

218
00:17:12,847 --> 00:17:15,280
The blast going in the bottom of the furnace

219
00:17:15,281 --> 00:17:16,730
causes the coke to burn.

220
00:17:16,731 --> 00:17:20,900
Just like when you take a bellows and fan the fire in your fireplace,

221
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:26,320
this is the same effect that the compressed air has in the bottom of a blast furnace, and of course

222
00:17:26,321 --> 00:17:28,300
That's where the blast furnace gets its name.

223
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:39,020
We're standing on the Twerr platform.

224
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,280
It's where the hot gases are blown into the furnace.

225
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:49,260
Right now we have a wind volume of about 230,000 cubic feet per minute being blown in

226
00:17:49,261 --> 00:17:52,360
at a temperature of 2,300 degrees Fahrenheit.

227
00:17:52,361 --> 00:17:59,800
The compressed hot air meets the raw materials in the hearth, and a series of chemical and thermal reactions take place.

228
00:18:00,560 --> 00:18:02,500
The liquid iron descends to the bottom.

229
00:18:04,180 --> 00:18:11,140
And then, 12 times a day, the pneumatic steel drill, looking like a monster grasshopper,

230
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:15,480
bores 11 feet into the blast furnace to extract the molten material.

231
00:18:16,220 --> 00:18:18,280
This is where the fireworks really begin.

232
00:18:18,281 --> 00:18:24,223
Molten iron is coming out of the furnace. We call it hot metal.

233
00:18:24,224 --> 00:18:28,280
It's 95% iron, 4% carbon, 1% gang material.

234
00:18:28,281 --> 00:18:34,280
The spent tap hole is plugged and the entire operation moves to the other side of the furnace.

235
00:18:34,281 --> 00:18:45,280
The hot metal is transported by refractory lined rail cars, called pew or torpedo cars, to a basic oxygen furnace about a mile away.

236
00:18:45,281 --> 00:18:46,224
a mile away.

237
00:18:46,225 --> 00:18:52,280
There, the purified liquid iron will be combined with scrap metal and alloys,

238
00:18:52,820 --> 00:18:58,820
subjected to a supersonic blast of pure oxygen and further processed into industrial steel.

239
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:04,979
What makes No. 7 Blast Furnace a world-class furnace is the size of this facility.

240
00:19:04,980 --> 00:19:05,680
It has

241
00:19:05,681 --> 00:19:12,340
a hearth diameter of 45 feet, which means we could put two blast furnaces of World War

242
00:19:12,341 --> 00:19:14,940
too vintage inside of number seven blast furnace.

243
00:19:15,760 --> 00:19:19,200
It has 40 tweer openings in the bottom of the furnace,

244
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:22,200
where most furnaces have half that amount.

245
00:19:22,920 --> 00:19:24,280
It has four tap holes.

246
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:28,020
It is capable of making 11,000 tons a day.

247
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:31,340
And it does this with the same number of people

248
00:19:31,341 --> 00:19:35,600
that was required to run one of the smaller blast furnaces

249
00:19:35,601 --> 00:19:37,040
over 50 years ago.

250
00:19:38,620 --> 00:19:42,140
This big blast furnace incorporates the latest in technology.

251
00:19:42,340 --> 00:19:48,520
Unlike furnaces of decades past, most of this streamlined operation is controlled remotely.

252
00:19:49,220 --> 00:19:51,700
This is number seven blast furnace control room.

253
00:19:52,160 --> 00:19:54,080
We receive all communications in here.

254
00:19:54,300 --> 00:19:58,660
We weigh and batch materials on a remote basis and charge to the furnace.

255
00:19:59,360 --> 00:20:01,460
We monitor all the process variables.

256
00:20:01,980 --> 00:20:03,820
We heat and fire the stoves.

257
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:09,960
We also control the grinding and injection of the pulverized coal injection going into the furnace.

258
00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:15,760
The computer screen is a mimic of the material being discharged out of the stockhouse way bends,

259
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:18,700
traveling up the primary conveyor belt.

260
00:20:19,240 --> 00:20:23,620
It's then charged to the furnace, and we do that cycling about 500 times per day.

261
00:20:24,220 --> 00:20:29,420
This entire operation runs day in and day out 365 days a year,

262
00:20:29,421 --> 00:20:34,640
with an annual liquid iron production rate of over 3.5 million tons.

263
00:20:37,060 --> 00:20:39,580
But number seven is getting some competition.

264
00:20:39,960 --> 00:20:45,480
In China and Korea, they are building new blast furnaces the size of number seven,

265
00:20:45,920 --> 00:20:48,640
because they are going through an industrial revolution

266
00:20:48,641 --> 00:20:53,840
and do not have enough steel to support the increase of infrastructure

267
00:20:53,841 --> 00:20:59,000
that is required to build bridges, highways, buildings, and their factories.

268
00:20:59,380 --> 00:21:02,040
But because of the enormous productivity levels,

269
00:21:02,041 --> 00:21:06,840
the number of blast furnaces in the U.S. has decreased dramatically.

270
00:21:06,841 --> 00:21:18,000
In 1946, there were 249 blast furnaces in operation, and in 2003, there are down to 33 blast furnaces.

271
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:26,780
However, I suspect within the last 30 years, the total amount of iron produced in the United States has actually grown,

272
00:21:27,360 --> 00:21:30,820
even though the number of blast furnaces has gone down.

273
00:21:30,821 --> 00:21:37,720
This is because production is getting concentrated now in larger furnaces, which have higher production efficiency.

274
00:21:38,380 --> 00:21:46,640
And the very largest and most productive of them all, number seven, lives here at this 19,000-acre steel-making facility,

275
00:21:48,100 --> 00:21:51,420
dedicated to the output of some seriously heavy metal.

276
00:21:52,340 --> 00:21:57,360
The same metal used to build the world's biggest marine diesel engines.

277
00:21:57,361 --> 00:22:05,260
150 years ago, a blast furnace made 7 tons of liquid iron in one day

278
00:22:05,261 --> 00:22:11,480
Today, number 7 can make 7 tons of liquid iron in one minute

