281
00:22:23,081 --> 00:22:31,320
This diesel engine has more than enough muscle to power an 80,000-pound fully loaded big rig.

282
00:22:32,960 --> 00:22:36,520
But it's got nothing on the Bronco that powers this vessel.

283
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:47,780
It's thousands of times larger than a car engine, more powerful than 200 semis, and able to harness the power of nearly 100,000 horses.

284
00:22:47,781 --> 00:22:54,240
It's K98MC and it is one mammoth marine diesel engine.

285
00:22:55,600 --> 00:23:00,700
Man B&W K98MC marine diesel engine.

286
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:03,460
Cylinders up to 14.

287
00:23:04,740 --> 00:23:08,660
Maximum dry weight 2,446 tons.

288
00:23:09,820 --> 00:23:14,220
Maximum power 108,688 horsepower.

289
00:23:14,221 --> 00:23:20,900
maximum fuel consumption 328 tons per day

290
00:23:22,640 --> 00:23:28,860
the massive K98 MC is among the biggest diesel engines in the world each

291
00:23:28,861 --> 00:23:34,640
customizable engine takes six months to build assemble and test because of its

292
00:23:34,641 --> 00:23:39,540
tremendous weight it has to be disassembled into six or seven pieces and

293
00:23:39,541 --> 00:23:42,200
and transported to the ship to be built up again.

294
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:49,180
The K-98 MC engine is the brute force behind the Lionsgate Bridge container ship.

295
00:23:51,020 --> 00:23:54,120
Lionsgate is a post-Panamax vessel.

296
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:57,080
In other words, she cannot go through the Panama Canal.

297
00:23:57,220 --> 00:23:58,160
She's too big for that.

298
00:23:59,220 --> 00:24:07,280
She's 940 feet long and 131 feet wide, which is pretty big, I would say.

299
00:24:07,281 --> 00:24:15,900
She can carry about 5,620-foot containers, which corresponds to about 60,000 tons of cargo.

300
00:24:16,500 --> 00:24:22,580
And if you look at the weight of the ship with its fuel and its cargo, you're carrying something about 95,000 tons.

301
00:24:25,740 --> 00:24:31,220
The engine on board the Lionsgate Bridge is an 11-cylinder model of the K-98MC.

302
00:24:32,260 --> 00:24:36,160
At nearly 2,000 tons, it's about 10% of the ship's weight.

303
00:24:37,280 --> 00:24:45,760
The engine spans 65 feet and you can only see the very top of the 40 foot workhorse from

304
00:24:45,761 --> 00:24:51,000
the highest floor of the engine room.

305
00:24:51,001 --> 00:24:55,780
The enormous pistons are 3 feet in diameter and can move 30 feet per second.

306
00:24:55,781 --> 00:25:02,280
It does that 94 revolutions per minute, 94 times per minute it's going up and down.

307
00:25:02,281 --> 00:25:10,640
It's very slow, really, but for this big engine, it's quite a big speed because it's moving up 10 feet and 10 feet down every time.

308
00:25:11,460 --> 00:25:14,920
The weight of it is almost 6 tons going up and down.

309
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:23,740
Like with all diesel engines, the piston compresses the air inside the cylinder, causing it to become extremely hot.

310
00:25:24,700 --> 00:25:30,140
Fuel is injected, it ignites, and the explosion forces the piston back down.

311
00:25:30,141 --> 00:25:37,340
The exhaust valves open and the air intake ports on cover, allowing fresh pressurized air to enter the cylinder.

312
00:25:38,780 --> 00:25:42,040
This air is then compressed and the cycle repeats.

313
00:25:43,200 --> 00:25:47,240
The movement of the piston originates with a crankshaft found inside the Rumi engine.

314
00:25:48,440 --> 00:25:52,920
When there's a problem, engineers are sometimes required to step inside.

315
00:25:53,560 --> 00:25:55,260
We're now inside the big engine.

316
00:25:55,880 --> 00:25:57,860
What I'm actually charging here is the crankshaft.

317
00:25:57,861 --> 00:26:00,740
The big crankshaft, it weighs about 100 tons.

318
00:26:01,400 --> 00:26:04,160
They make them only in a few places in this world.

319
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:05,900
100 tons moving around here.

320
00:26:06,680 --> 00:26:07,880
This is the connection rod.

321
00:26:08,440 --> 00:26:10,040
It's about five or six tons.

322
00:26:10,940 --> 00:26:13,000
When the engine is running, I couldn't be in here.

323
00:26:13,560 --> 00:26:16,880
This would be all filled with oil and all the mass is moving around.

324
00:26:16,980 --> 00:26:19,660
So this is no place for people to be when the engine is running.

325
00:26:20,440 --> 00:26:22,340
But there's no stopping this engine.

326
00:26:22,900 --> 00:26:27,080
When problems arise, one cylinder can be taken out of service to be repaired,

327
00:26:27,081 --> 00:26:28,960
while the others continue working.

328
00:26:29,520 --> 00:26:33,900
You could put about 150 truck engines in this ship and get the same power,

329
00:26:34,640 --> 00:26:36,180
but they would not last the first year.

330
00:26:36,660 --> 00:26:39,260
This engine runs about 6,000 hours per year,

331
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:41,600
so it's actually running most of the time,

332
00:26:42,080 --> 00:26:45,260
and this kind of reliability you don't find in a truck engine.

333
00:26:45,840 --> 00:26:48,300
The speed and power of the vessel are controlled

334
00:26:48,301 --> 00:26:51,060
by the varying amounts of fuel injected into the cylinder.

335
00:26:52,840 --> 00:26:56,120
We pump in about 200 tons of fuel per day,

336
00:26:56,121 --> 00:26:58,360
200 tons of heavy fuel oil.

337
00:26:59,100 --> 00:27:00,620
This sounds like an awful lot,

338
00:27:01,300 --> 00:27:02,460
but if you think about it,

339
00:27:02,940 --> 00:27:04,900
a big truck would pump in the double

340
00:27:04,901 --> 00:27:06,440
to make the same amount of horsepower.

341
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:09,680
So this is a very, very fuel-efficient engine.

342
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:12,140
Not only is it very fuel-efficient,

343
00:27:12,480 --> 00:27:15,220
it burns some of the heaviest fuel oil

344
00:27:15,221 --> 00:27:16,480
you can think of in the world.

345
00:27:17,200 --> 00:27:18,500
And to pack more punch,

346
00:27:18,501 --> 00:27:21,000
the engine contains three turbochargers.

347
00:27:22,260 --> 00:27:24,360
These turbos boost the engine's horsepower.

348
00:27:26,120 --> 00:27:33,240
That power is harnessed to turn the massive propeller, which moves the Titanic-sized ship across the ocean effortlessly.

349
00:27:34,140 --> 00:27:37,620
Plus, we can start it from the bridge, which is what we normally do.

350
00:27:38,160 --> 00:27:39,720
We can start it from control room.

351
00:27:40,280 --> 00:27:46,300
And there is a local mechanical system also where we bypass all the computers in case all the computers crash.

352
00:27:46,920 --> 00:27:49,100
We can just start it mechanically and it will go.

353
00:27:50,300 --> 00:27:53,780
Once the engines are turned on, the ship is raring to go.

354
00:27:53,781 --> 00:27:56,393
You know, it's like having horses underneath you.

355
00:27:56,394 --> 00:27:59,060
You just kick them up and they start moving ahead.

356
00:27:59,460 --> 00:28:01,820
So this is the power of this engine here.

357
00:28:02,640 --> 00:28:08,400
With 11 massive cylinders and a potential to add up to three more, this engine can haul.

358
00:28:08,960 --> 00:28:11,420
Other ships are normally 23, 24 knots.

359
00:28:12,140 --> 00:28:15,900
And this one, if I open up the full power, it is 30 nautical miles.

360
00:28:16,120 --> 00:28:17,520
So we are much faster than them.

361
00:28:17,660 --> 00:28:23,120
It's like sitting in a Ferrari on a racetrack and we can overtake any ship in the world, any ship.

362
00:28:23,780 --> 00:28:31,720
The K-98MC and all diesel engines owe their efficient design to the ingenuity of one man, Rudolf Diesel.

363
00:28:32,660 --> 00:28:36,840
He was an engineer, and he actually calculated this type of engine.

364
00:28:37,380 --> 00:28:39,340
At that time, it was the end of the 18th century.

365
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:46,340
It was all steam engines, but he wanted to make a more efficient engine, so he came up with the diesel engine principle.

366
00:28:46,820 --> 00:28:49,420
His first engine was 1893.

367
00:28:49,421 --> 00:28:59,160
And today, with larger ships entering the sea, mammoth marine diesel engines like the K98MC will be proliferating.

368
00:28:59,900 --> 00:29:06,020
The ships are going to be longer, they're going to be wider, but the marvel of it is that these same engines are going to power her across.

369
00:29:06,940 --> 00:29:16,520
Diesel engines may be able to move mountains, but it takes DC motors to power another of the world's biggest machines, a colossal convertible roof.

370
00:29:16,521 --> 00:29:28,240
With engines like the K98MC, large container ships are able to cross the Atlantic Ocean in 6 days and the Pacific Ocean in 10 days.

371
00:29:29,140 --> 00:29:35,240
World's Biggest Machines will return on Modern Marvels, here on the History Channel.

372
00:29:39,100 --> 00:29:42,980
We now return to World's Biggest Machines on Modern Marvels.

373
00:29:46,520 --> 00:29:48,180
Everybody loves a convertible.

374
00:29:49,700 --> 00:29:51,640
With just the flip of a switch,

375
00:29:52,300 --> 00:29:54,240
convertibles let you enjoy the outdoors

376
00:29:54,241 --> 00:29:56,560
while staying comfortably inside.

377
00:29:57,180 --> 00:29:58,640
It's the best of both worlds.

378
00:29:59,640 --> 00:30:04,240
In 1989, the city of Toronto kicked it up one giant notch

379
00:30:04,241 --> 00:30:07,160
with the world's first fully convertible stadium.

380
00:30:08,440 --> 00:30:10,720
They called it Skydome.

381
00:30:11,780 --> 00:30:15,140
And what makes it all possible is a retractable roof

382
00:30:15,141 --> 00:30:17,440
It's one of the world's biggest machines.

383
00:30:19,240 --> 00:30:21,100
Skydome retractable roof.

384
00:30:21,780 --> 00:30:24,520
Height, approximately 300 feet.

385
00:30:25,300 --> 00:30:27,220
Weight, 11,000 tons.

386
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:30,780
Roof span, 680 feet.

387
00:30:31,980 --> 00:30:33,940
Roof area, 8 acres.

388
00:30:35,320 --> 00:30:37,900
A truly remarkable feat of design and engineering.

389
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:43,620
The retractable roof never fails to attract cheers as it glides back to reveal the heavens.

390
00:30:43,621 --> 00:30:47,960
It's an 11,000-ton structure that's opening and closing.

391
00:30:47,961 --> 00:30:52,740
And when it's fully open, the entire playing surface is under direct sunlight,

392
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:56,040
and over 90% of the seats are also under direct sunlight.

393
00:30:56,260 --> 00:31:00,440
So it really transforms it into a different type of stadium,

394
00:31:00,580 --> 00:31:02,880
a different atmosphere, a different feel in the stadium.

395
00:31:03,460 --> 00:31:08,840
And I don't think that there'll ever be a day where anyone will look at that roof opening or closing and be bored by it.

396
00:31:08,841 --> 00:31:20,585
One of the things that's never really talked about very much is that if you can put a retractable roof on a baseball stadium, for instance, that works, you don't have

397
00:31:20,586 --> 00:31:22,920
any more rain days. They're gone.

398
00:31:23,680 --> 00:31:32,060
In the mid-1980s, frustrated by more and more cold and rained out sporting events, Toronto city officials decided to build an indoor stadium.

399
00:31:32,061 --> 00:31:35,220
Toronto is in the northern climate.

400
00:31:35,520 --> 00:31:38,640
We have terrible falls with snowfalls as early as November.

401
00:31:39,300 --> 00:31:40,920
Spring is equally awkward.

402
00:31:41,460 --> 00:31:44,900
So the thought was, well, why don't we get a dome stadium for Toronto?

403
00:31:45,340 --> 00:31:48,500
But then the baseball crowd started to raise their objections,

404
00:31:48,501 --> 00:31:53,220
say, well, yeah, it would be nice to play under a fixed roof when the weather is bad.

405
00:31:53,580 --> 00:31:56,520
But, you know, baseball, it's meant for the open airs.

406
00:31:56,540 --> 00:31:57,340
It's a summer sport.

407
00:31:58,180 --> 00:31:59,580
To make it financially viable,

408
00:32:00,180 --> 00:32:03,580
the arena had to be able to accommodate football and other events as well.

409
00:32:05,160 --> 00:32:07,600
A round, convertible, domed stadium.

410
00:32:08,500 --> 00:32:11,360
It was a project the likes of which had never been attempted.

411
00:32:12,480 --> 00:32:14,880
The real dilemma was going to be the mechanics.

412
00:32:15,720 --> 00:32:16,660
How would it work?

413
00:32:17,580 --> 00:32:22,700
It was up to structural engineer Mike Allen and architect Rod Robby to figure that out.

414
00:32:23,360 --> 00:32:28,560
It's a question of engineering science, but it's also a question of solid geometry.

415
00:32:29,580 --> 00:32:37,720
They sat at the drawing board for months and finally came up with a plan that was simple, brilliant and controversial.

416
00:32:38,860 --> 00:32:48,800
If you take that circular dome and imagine it as like half a grapefruit upside down and slice it into four segments,

417
00:32:49,500 --> 00:32:54,360
the two middle segments are almost rectangular and they could indeed slide back.

418
00:32:55,320 --> 00:32:58,920
The panel on the far end was like a quarter of a sphere,

419
00:32:59,200 --> 00:33:01,960
and it could slide on a circular track system.

420
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:03,240
So that was the trick.

421
00:33:04,220 --> 00:33:07,940
The next trick was convincing everyone that this idea could really work.

422
00:33:08,180 --> 00:33:15,060
We put together a video which demonstrated the design approach

423
00:33:15,061 --> 00:33:17,260
and how the roof opened and closed.

424
00:33:17,680 --> 00:33:20,600
It was a very crude video done with CAD drafting techniques,

425
00:33:20,760 --> 00:33:24,240
which in those days was state-of-the-art, but today would be considered crude.

426
00:33:24,360 --> 00:33:28,620
but it did the job of describing visually how these panels would work.

427
00:33:30,060 --> 00:33:33,860
To further demonstrate their plan, they even built a working scale model.

428
00:33:34,420 --> 00:33:39,060
Even though we've done all this testing on the computer, there's nothing like a real model.

429
00:33:39,520 --> 00:33:43,400
That particular model cost us about $100,000 to build.

430
00:33:43,401 --> 00:33:47,720
It was a very expensive model to build, but it was worth it.

431
00:33:47,980 --> 00:33:50,620
It really helped us prove that this thing would work.

432
00:33:50,621 --> 00:33:55,620
Construction of the Sky Dome Stadium began in the fall of 1986.

433
00:33:55,621 --> 00:33:59,820
Crews worked day and night through cold harsh winters

434
00:33:59,821 --> 00:34:05,520
and blazing hot summers. Once the colossal framework was up,

435
00:34:05,521 --> 00:34:09,120
the roof structure was covered with a corrugated steel deck.

436
00:34:09,121 --> 00:34:14,620
Finally, acoustic and thermal insulation was installed

437
00:34:14,621 --> 00:34:18,520
and the entire roof was sealed with a polyvinyl chloride skin

438
00:34:18,521 --> 00:34:21,000
to secure the climate-controlled environment.

439
00:34:23,940 --> 00:34:29,920
The 2.5 million square foot superstructure was completed in under three years

440
00:34:29,921 --> 00:34:33,280
at a cost of over $500 million.

441
00:34:35,120 --> 00:34:37,540
From the outside, the Skydome looked great.

442
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:40,460
But the question on everybody's mind was still,

443
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,320
would the convertible roof actually work?

444
00:34:43,840 --> 00:34:45,680
Well, of course, we have our doubts.

445
00:34:45,681 --> 00:34:46,900
It would have put us out of business.

446
00:34:46,901 --> 00:34:49,960
would have been tens of millions of dollars worth of problems.

447
00:34:50,140 --> 00:34:54,480
Aside from the top-notch design team and construction team we put on the job,

448
00:34:54,920 --> 00:34:57,840
in all the meetings and interaction, we hired experts all over the world.

449
00:34:58,920 --> 00:35:03,580
On June 3, 1989, plagued by skepticism and criticism,

450
00:35:04,640 --> 00:35:07,400
the Skydome was finally ready for its moment of truth.

451
00:35:09,600 --> 00:35:12,480
In front of an intimate crowd of 60,000,

452
00:35:12,880 --> 00:35:15,920
Rod Robby, Mike Allen, and the entire build team

453
00:35:15,921 --> 00:35:19,700
watched with anticipation to see if their roof would open.

454
00:35:20,400 --> 00:35:25,160
Four, three, two, one.

455
00:35:34,860 --> 00:35:38,320
And, you know, I think I said to Michael at the time,

456
00:35:38,720 --> 00:35:41,960
it's the only time I'd seen a building congratulated for operating.

457
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:44,120
You know, it was really quite spectacular.

458
00:35:45,920 --> 00:35:53,960
People think of the engineers as being very methodical and mathematical and kind of cold, but we have a heart too.

459
00:35:54,120 --> 00:35:56,220
And it was a very moving experience.

460
00:35:57,020 --> 00:36:02,980
Since that day, the Skydome has successfully raised the roof, so to speak, over and over.

461
00:36:04,740 --> 00:36:11,240
The roof structure operates on a series of steel tracks and 54 drive mechanisms called bogeys.

462
00:36:11,241 --> 00:36:17,800
the three moving roof panels are powered by a total of 76 dc motors

463
00:36:17,801 --> 00:36:21,220
the generator combines 750 horsepower

464
00:36:21,221 --> 00:36:27,200
not a lot of muscle when you consider the colossal mass that it moves

465
00:36:27,201 --> 00:36:33,400
and when it's time to throw it into action it's as simple as a flip of a switch almost

466
00:36:33,401 --> 00:36:39,260
when this roof opens up people go crazy it's amazing you know you're inside a concrete

467
00:36:39,261 --> 00:36:44,153
building and then the big convertible just opens right up and everybody loves it.

468
00:36:44,154 --> 00:36:45,180
It's unbelievable

469
00:36:45,181 --> 00:36:50,080
when you see all this steel and unison move across nicely and slide into position.

470
00:36:51,100 --> 00:36:53,382
You can't help but be awed by it.

471
00:36:53,383 --> 00:36:57,600
It is so safe and simple and that for thereafter you're going

472
00:36:57,601 --> 00:37:02,720
to have a stadium that is going to offer the perfect solution for any weather condition.

473
00:37:02,980 --> 00:37:05,848
You know when I come back here I am still amazed by the building.

474
00:37:05,849 --> 00:37:07,260
You can't help but being amazed.

475
00:37:07,261 --> 00:37:08,440
I love this building.

476
00:37:09,120 --> 00:37:11,980
The Skydome is an awesome machine built by man.

477
00:37:12,720 --> 00:37:15,520
But some machines build machines.

478
00:37:16,300 --> 00:37:18,400
Get ready for the world's biggest robots.

479
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:26,120
It takes less than $10 worth of electricity to open or close the Skydome roof.

480
00:37:27,060 --> 00:37:30,420
World's biggest machines will return on Modern Marvels.

481
00:37:30,840 --> 00:37:32,720
Here on the History Channel.

482
00:37:32,721 --> 00:37:39,720
We now return to World's Biggest Machines on Modern Marvels.

483
00:37:53,720 --> 00:38:01,720
Years after robots took over the Earth, they are now conquering a new frontier, outer space.

484
00:38:02,720 --> 00:38:06,400
And the giant of them all currently resides there.

485
00:38:08,000 --> 00:38:13,100
Space Station Remote Manipulator System, a.k.a. Canadarm2.

486
00:38:14,680 --> 00:38:18,420
Length, 57.7 feet fully extended.

487
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:22,700
Weight on Earth, nearly 4,000 pounds.

488
00:38:23,900 --> 00:38:26,140
Useful life, 15 years.

489
00:38:27,360 --> 00:38:30,120
Max payloads, 125 tons.

490
00:38:30,121 --> 00:38:35,600
The Canadarm 2 is completely installed on its own right.

491
00:38:36,160 --> 00:38:39,100
It was launched in April 2001.

492
00:38:39,560 --> 00:38:45,360
It deployed itself and then it became a robotic arm on the side of the station which could move around.

493
00:38:46,880 --> 00:38:51,760
Multiple sensors on the arm provide an intelligent connection with computer-based controllers,

494
00:38:52,500 --> 00:38:54,360
distinguishing it from common machines.

495
00:38:55,680 --> 00:38:57,880
However, it is not autonomous.

496
00:38:57,881 --> 00:39:03,880
After all, humans are not yet ready to lose full command of the two-ton, 50-foot giant

497
00:39:03,881 --> 00:39:06,880
that is capable of lifting large, heavy objects.

498
00:39:06,881 --> 00:39:14,880
The control of the Candidarm 2 is from astronauts within the laboratory part of the International Space Station.

499
00:39:14,881 --> 00:39:21,880
They're sat there with hand controllers and they're watching video screens and telemetry data that comes back from the arm.

500
00:39:21,881 --> 00:39:31,880
The gigantic arm executes a variety of superhuman actions, including assembly, maintenance and servicing of the International Space Station.

501
00:39:32,880 --> 00:39:37,880
And it's able to traverse the length of a football field by two means.

502
00:39:38,880 --> 00:39:46,880
One being the mobile base system, a platform set on rails that can cart Canadarm2 laterally along the space station.

503
00:39:46,881 --> 00:39:51,380
and the other by inchworming its way across.

504
00:39:52,120 --> 00:39:58,020
When Canada Arm 2 attached to the station, it attached to a power data grapple fixture.

505
00:39:58,340 --> 00:40:07,000
And once the arm attaches to a PDGF, it gets power, video, and data and commands through that link.

506
00:40:07,460 --> 00:40:10,320
But both ends of the arm have the same mechanisms.

507
00:40:10,321 --> 00:40:17,280
And so we can then maneuver the arm such that the free end can attach to a second PDGF,

508
00:40:17,380 --> 00:40:19,120
and then we can transfer the base.

509
00:40:19,620 --> 00:40:24,500
And so we can move ourselves around the station, if you like, end over end,

510
00:40:24,620 --> 00:40:26,980
or inchworming as people like to call it.

511
00:40:27,940 --> 00:40:32,980
The Canadarm2 has seven mechanized joints, allowing it this degree of freedom.

512
00:40:34,340 --> 00:40:39,240
Besides being versatile and flexible, the giant robotic arm is also durable.

513
00:40:39,241 --> 00:40:47,640
Canadarm 2, once it was launched and put onto the station, is going to remain there for the life of the station and the life of the program.

514
00:40:48,240 --> 00:40:51,140
It will never return to Earth during its useful life.

515
00:40:51,820 --> 00:40:58,240
Still, Canadarm 2 remains a remote relative of the industrial robots that currently toil far below.

516
00:40:58,241 --> 00:41:05,540
Of the over 800,000 robots that inhabit the Earth, 90% are industrial robots.

517
00:41:06,240 --> 00:41:09,560
And one of the largest on Earth is expanding its resume.

518
00:41:12,060 --> 00:41:13,680
KR 500.

519
00:41:14,660 --> 00:41:17,940
Max payload, 1,250 pounds.

520
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:21,800
Height, including base, 22 feet.

521
00:41:22,700 --> 00:41:25,720
Weight, including base, nearly 4 tons.

522
00:41:25,721 --> 00:41:29,260
operating system Microsoft Windows.

523
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:33,440
The KR500 can be found in the factory

524
00:41:33,441 --> 00:41:37,440
with a 50% chance that it's an automobile factory.

525
00:41:38,520 --> 00:41:40,980
Basically, you've got the largest robot

526
00:41:40,981 --> 00:41:45,200
that can move half a ton incredibly fast

527
00:41:45,201 --> 00:41:48,520
and put it in a very precise location.

528
00:41:49,000 --> 00:41:50,920
This is something that could never be done before.

529
00:41:52,040 --> 00:41:54,480
The Six Axes KR500

530
00:41:54,481 --> 00:41:57,220
has taken robots to a new level within the industry.

531
00:41:58,260 --> 00:42:01,540
A world far different than that of the first industrial robot,

532
00:42:02,080 --> 00:42:05,100
the Unimane, which was introduced in 1961.

533
00:42:06,440 --> 00:42:09,340
When a robot first came out, there were all sorts of predictions

534
00:42:09,341 --> 00:42:11,640
as to what a robot could do, what it couldn't do.

535
00:42:11,860 --> 00:42:15,380
Many people were afraid of it, that it was going to take away jobs.

536
00:42:15,720 --> 00:42:19,820
Many people thought that robots were able to do all sorts of wonderful things,

537
00:42:19,821 --> 00:42:23,420
but the computing power and the fact that they were originally hydraulic

538
00:42:23,421 --> 00:42:24,663
wasn't quite there yet.

539
00:42:24,664 --> 00:42:28,500
When the hydraulics were removed and when they became servomotor-driven

540
00:42:28,501 --> 00:42:34,120
and when PC-based robotics came out and the PC was able to actually control the machine

541
00:42:34,121 --> 00:42:39,060
instead of dedicated controllers, robots became much more powerful and able to do things

542
00:42:39,061 --> 00:42:41,620
that people originally wanted early on when they first came out.

543
00:42:42,420 --> 00:42:48,960
The KR500 is autonomous, reprogrammable, and can lift a half ton ten feet into the air,

544
00:42:49,600 --> 00:42:54,740
twist it 360 degrees, and place it down with 0.3 millimeter precision.

545
00:42:57,260 --> 00:43:00,120
Once isolated from humans on the factory floor,

546
00:43:00,920 --> 00:43:04,340
the powerful KR500 is now embracing them.

547
00:43:07,320 --> 00:43:10,220
The Robocoaster came from the KR500.

548
00:43:10,920 --> 00:43:12,720
When KUKA developed the KR500,

549
00:43:12,721 --> 00:43:16,700
one of our people out of the U.K. office named Gino de Gaulle said,

550
00:43:16,701 --> 00:43:19,220
I think I can make a ride out of this.

551
00:43:19,600 --> 00:43:23,020
He always dreamed of having someone ride a robot.

552
00:43:23,820 --> 00:43:28,300
In November 2003, after 24 months of extensive testing,

553
00:43:29,040 --> 00:43:34,700
this larger-than-life robotic arm successfully made the transition into the recreation industry,

554
00:43:35,660 --> 00:43:38,040
becoming its first passenger-carrying robot.

555
00:43:39,780 --> 00:43:45,740
With seven programmed intensity levels and 1.4 million possible movement combinations,

556
00:43:46,700 --> 00:43:49,780
riders are able to determine their own robotic experience.

557
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:55,480
And just one year after its debut,

558
00:43:56,620 --> 00:44:00,580
the KR500 is now on the fast track.

559
00:44:02,100 --> 00:44:05,000
We're now into a new ride profile.

560
00:44:05,340 --> 00:44:08,560
It's called Robocoaster G2, Generation 2.

561
00:44:08,561 --> 00:44:11,040
So what we did is we took the KR500,

562
00:44:11,500 --> 00:44:13,740
we took off Axis 5 and Axis 6,

563
00:44:14,080 --> 00:44:16,480
we pulled the center of gravity back on the robot,

564
00:44:16,700 --> 00:44:19,160
mounted four chairs on the end,

565
00:44:19,340 --> 00:44:21,140
and that is mounted on a roller coaster track.

566
00:44:23,840 --> 00:44:27,320
From the KR500, robots will get bigger.

567
00:44:28,320 --> 00:44:31,640
You will have, instead of cranes and other types of operations

568
00:44:31,641 --> 00:44:34,120
where you've got people moving in some sort of machine,

569
00:44:34,620 --> 00:44:38,720
those will eventually be replaced with the equivalent in a robot form.

570
00:44:39,780 --> 00:44:43,180
Maybe robots haven't conquered the world yet,

571
00:44:43,440 --> 00:44:46,640
but there's no doubt that they're poised and ready to do it.

572
00:44:46,700 --> 00:44:47,980
in a big way.

